start-cosmic assumes XDG_RUNTIME_DIR already exists, having been created
earlier by pam_systemd/pam_elogind during login. That holds when COSMIC is
started from a greeter (cosmic-greeter, lightdm, ...), since the greeter's
PAM session sets it up first. It does not hold when start-cosmic is invoked
from a shell profile (e.g. ~/.zprofile) on a manual TTY login, where
/run/user/$UID has not yet been created -- cosmic-comp then panics (exit
code 101) trying to create its Wayland socket in a directory that does not
exist, and restart-loops silently because .zprofile does not surface stderr
anywhere visible.

Bootstrap XDG_RUNTIME_DIR when it is unset or points to a missing directory,
instead of trusting that something else already created it. This is a no-op
on setups where a greeter already did the job correctly, and only creates the
directory when it is actually absent.

--- a/data/start-cosmic
+++ b/data/start-cosmic
@@ -3,6 +3,13 @@
 set -e

 # From: https://people.debian.org/~mpitt/systemd.conf-2016-graphical-session.pdf
+
+# Bootstrap XDG_RUNTIME_DIR if whatever started this session (e.g. a
+# manual TTY login via ~/.zprofile, without a greeter) didn't already
+# set it up via pam_elogind/pam_systemd. cosmic-comp panics if this
+# directory doesn't exist when it tries to create its Wayland socket.
+export XDG_RUNTIME_DIR="${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/run/user/$(id -u)}"
+[ -d "${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR}" ] || mkdir -p -m 0700 "${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR}"

 if command -v systemctl >/dev/null; then
     # robustness: if the previous graphical session left some failed units,
